WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.530
- [Narrator] Plague is
notorious for causing

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:05.823
mass sickness and devastation.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.393
But as much tragedy as
the disease has caused,

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:15.933
it also helped drive crucial
scientific and social progress.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.610
Plague is an infectious disease

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:25.130
caused by the bacterium Yersinia pestis.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:29.370
It mainly affects rodents,
and spreads by way of insects.

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:31.440
Because of these insect carriers,

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.270
plague has been passed onto humans

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.363
with devastating consequences.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:41.400
Three major plague pandemics
have occurred in human history.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.530
And while they occurred centuries apart,

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:44.780
they shared similar traits

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.393
that paved the way for
the spread of disease.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.440
One cause of plague pandemics

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.330
was the rise of international trade.

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:56.220
Trade routes connected
once-isolated communities

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.610
and created large economic networks.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.200
But by facilitating the movement

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.060
of goods between communities,

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:06.633
trade routes also facilitated
the movement of germs.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.440
International trade was an impetus

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.830
for the first plague pandemic on record,

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:13.563
the Plague of Justinian.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:17.590
In the sixth century,
outbreaks began in Egypt

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:20.330
and, thanks to land and sea trade routes,

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:23.370
they spread throughout
the Byzantine Empire.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:25.400
Named after the emperor at the time,

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.370
the Plague of Justinian is
estimated to have wiped out

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.633
about half of Europe's population.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.160
Growing economies also
made way for urbanization

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.440
and a rising urban population.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:43.500
This resulted in crowded neighborhoods

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.530
and the accumulation of waste,

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:49.090
which created unsanitary
living conditions.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:52.060
Cities and their residents
essentially became incubators

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:53.693
for germs and diseases.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:58.480
This was particularly
evident in the second

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.360
and most infamous plague pandemic.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:02.960
In the 14th century,

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.600
Europe was experiencing an
economic and population boom,

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.720
especially in cities.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.490
Proper waste management
did not exist at the time,

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.673
making cities vulnerable to disease.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.350
After trade routes
brought plague from Asia,

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.380
where it killed millions in
China and the Middle East,

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:24.290
the disease wiped out about a
third of Europe's population,

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:27.553
earning itself the
moniker the Black Death.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:35.320
What also aided in the
transmission of the disease

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:38.020
was the lack of medical knowledge.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:39.740
For most of human history,

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:42.950
the cause of illnesses,
germs, was unknown,

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.470
making sicknesses like
the plague a mystery.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:49.010
This lack of knowledge
drove the spread of disease

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:52.030
as recently as the 19th century.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.820
Outbreaks in northwest India

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:57.210
eventually reached major
port cities in China.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.970
In just over a century,

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:01.350
plague was exported throughout the globe

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:05.423
and caused outbreaks in every
continent except Antarctica,

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.540
making it the most widespread
pandemic in history.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:13.483
This plague pandemic,
however, was the last.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:19.010
In 1894, scientists
discovered the bacteria

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.170
behind the plague outbreaks.

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:24.760
Their discovery helped further
developments in microbiology,

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:28.060
medicine, urban planning,
and sanitation methods,

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:31.103
which led to the treatment
and prevention of the disease.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:36.850
Economic expansion, urbanization,

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:39.060
and a lack of medical knowledge

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.073
contributed to the
disastrous spread of plague.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.380
In turn, however, the
disease helped catapult

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.840
crucial advancements in
science and public health,

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.543
very well making plague
pandemics a thing of the past.

